issue.body:
When I used an executable built from a java_binary from skylark, it fails complaining that the runfiles directory is not available. This is because Bazel builds the wrapper script for the java_binary target but not the runfiles directory.

However if I use a genrule with the same java_binary executable it works fine. This seems like a problem with skylark.

See the following example:

=== BUILD ===

```
load("test", "test")

java_library(
  name = "test",
  srcs = ["TestCase.java"],
)

java_binary(
  name = "test_app",
  runtime_deps = [":test"],
  main_class = "TestCase",
)

test(name = "test_txt")

genrule(
  name = "test_genrule",
  tools = [":test_app"],
  outs = ["test_txt2"],
  cmd = "$(location :test_app) > $@",
)
```

=== test.bzl ===

```
def test_impl(ctx):
  ctx.action(
    outputs = [ctx.outputs.txt],
    mnemonic = "TestStep",
    inputs = [ctx.executable._test_app],
    command = "%s > %s" % (ctx.executable._test_app.path, ctx.outputs.txt.path),
    use_default_shell_env = True)
  return struct()

test = rule(
  test_impl,
  attrs = {
    "_test_app": attr.label(executable=True, default=Label("//:test_app"), allow_files=True),
  },
  outputs = {"txt": "lib%{name}.txt"}
)
```

=== TestCase.java ===

```
class TestCase {
    public TestCase() {
    }

    public static void main(String [] args) {
        System.out.println("test case");
    }
}
```
